verses.text:
“Many nights, as I lay in bed,{a}
I searched for the man my heart loves.
I searched, and could not find him.

Who is this coming up from the desert{b}
like pillars of smoke,
smelling of myrrh and frankincense,{c}
and of all the spices a trader sells?

“You are so lovely, my dearest!{a}
Truly lovely!
Your eyes are doves
behind your veil.
Your hair ripples like a herd of goats
coming down the slopes of Gilead.{b}

Leave Lebanon and come with me, my bride.{d}
Leave Lebanon behind.
Look down from the crest of Amana,
from the heights of Senir and of Hermon,{e}
from the lions’ dens
and the leopards’ hills.

nard and saffron,{i}
sweet cane{j} and cinnamon,
with every kind of incense tree,
myrrh and aloes,{k}
and all the best spices.

His head is polished gold.
His hair hangs in waves,{h}
black like a raven.

His arms are bars of gold
set with gems from Tarshish.{k}
His chest is smooth ivory
covered with blue stones.{l}

“You are as beautiful as Tirzah,{c} my dearest,
as lovely as Jerusalem,
as stunning as an army with its flags.{d}

Return, return, Shulammite!{h}
Return, return, so we may gaze at you!”
“Why would you gaze at the Shulammite
as she dances between two lines of dancers?”{i}

“How lovely your sandaled feet are,{a}
noble daughter!{b}
Your rounded hips look like jewelry,
crafted by an artist’s hands.

Your neck is an ivory tower.
Your eyes are like the pools of Heshbon{c}
near the gate of Bath-rabbim.{d}
Your nose is like the tower of Lebanon
that faces Damascus.

Your head rises over you like Mount Carmel.{e}
Your flowing hair shines like purple cloth.{f}
A king is held captive in its waves.

and your kisses be like the finest wine,
flowing smoothly for my love,{h}
moving over the lips of those who sleep.”{i}

Come, my love.
Let us walk out to the fields
and stay the night among the henna shrubs.{j}

I make you promise, women of Jerusalem:
why stir up love or wake it
before it is ready?”{c}

“Who is this woman coming up from the wilderness,{d}
leaning on her beloved?”
“I woke you under the apple tree.{e}
That is where your mother went into labor with you.
That is where she gave birth to you.

Wear me like a seal over your heart,
like a ring on your arm,
for love is as strong as death,
and its passion{f} is as firm as the grave.
Its sparks are sparks of fire,
a flame of Yah.{g}

“I have been a wall,{i}
and my breasts have been towers.
So he now looks at me
as one who has found peace.{j}

Solomon owned a vineyard at Baal-hamon.{k}
He rented the vineyard to farmers.
Each one had to pay him
twenty-five pounds of silver for its fruit.{l}

My own vineyard is mine to give.
Solomon, you may have the twenty-five pounds,
and the farmers who tend its fruit may have five pounds.”{m}

“You who sit in the gardens,{n}
friends are waiting to hear your voice.
Let me be the one who hears it!”
